No Second Brain file is copied; each piece is restated for Rhythm's own engine, database and tests. A few short
passages do follow Second Brain's own wording (some sentences in the tool descriptions, the patterns that find
secrets and dates, and names such as tags, actions and tool arguments), and are used under the MIT License above:
- Keyword search (engine/src/Core/LexicalSearch.cs): matching any content word instead of every word, leaving out the
  function words a question is made of unless nothing else is left, and weighting each word by how rare it is among
  the candidates, after Second Brain's keyword arm (src/recall/search.ts, src/recall/fts.ts, src/text/tokenize.ts).
- Combined retrieval (engine/src/Core/Recall.cs): reciprocal rank fusion of keyword and meaning results with keyword
  weights from the rarity of the words a result holds, maximal marginal relevance in blocks of five, graph neighbours
  at the 5th and 10th places with a decay per hop, an output budget with larger excerpts for the leading matches, and
  a notice when semantic search is skipped, after Second Brain's recall (src/recall/rrf.ts, src/recall/search.ts,
  src/recall/math.ts, src/recall/snippet.ts, src/recall/neighborhood.ts, src/graph/traverse.ts).
- Time phrases (engine/src/Core/TimePhrases.cs): relative phrases ("yesterday", "this week", "last month") and a month
  and day that stands alone read in the install's time zone as a window on a record's time, with the guards that keep a
  name, an as-of date or a range from becoming one, after Second Brain's time phrases (src/text/temporal.ts).
- The retrieval gate (engine/tests/Knowledge.Tests/Parity/ParityGate*.cs): a gate whose rules are fixed before any
  run, a minimum sample, paired cluster bootstrap intervals, a minimum detectable effect, expected gaps kept out of the
  gains, and a locked baseline, after Second Brain's locked evaluation (test/eval/gate.ts, test/eval/stats.ts,
  test/eval/lock.ts, test/eval/corpus/build.ts).
- The memory scope contract (docs/dev/memory.md): an author lock, scope filters that only narrow, and history that
  starts when a memory is shared, after src/lib/entry-access.ts, src/lib/scope.ts and src/memory/history-visibility.ts.
- The embeddings index job (engine/src/Core/EmbeddingBackfill.cs): a keyset cursor kept per target model, so a run
  resumes after a stop and a change of model starts its own sweep; a per-run budget that always takes its first row;
  and a pause instead of a retry loop when nothing succeeds, after Second Brain's re-embedding run and pending-vector
  backfill (src/migration/embedding.ts, src/vectorize/pending.ts).
- The native memory store (engine/src/Core/Memories.cs, engine/migrations/033_memstore.sql): tags in the namespaces
  the engine sets itself left off and named, #hashtags taken as tags (all digits left alone), a limit of 131 072 bytes
  of UTF-8, an append as a dated update, new text clearing the volatility verdict, and a project a write names made
  when it is new, after Second Brain's write paths (src/tags/system.ts, src/tags/t7.ts, src/text/hashtags.ts,
  src/lib/content-size.ts, src/capture/entry.ts, src/capture/store.ts, src/projects/autocreate.ts).
- The project registry (engine/migrations/035_memory_projects.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryProjects.cs): one registry per
  scope, a slug that never changes once made, a name, a description, up to 16 aliases that claim plain tags (a memory of
  the project's scope tagged with one is a member, its tags not rewritten), archive that hides a project from the list
  unless asked, and delete that removes the grouping only, after Second Brain's project registry
  (src/projects/registry.ts); a Family project changed only by the person who made it or a Family admin, a slug or an
  alias never naming two projects of one scope, and a version kept of a memory whose project is deleted are Rhythm's
  own.
- A project's own view on the map (engine/src/Core/RelationshipEngine.cs): a project's members seeded from the links
  with a member at one end, after Second Brain's project graph (src/graph/traverse.ts); the project's star first, then
  the stars in it, then the stars they link to, each in the map's own order, are Rhythm's own.
- Holds on native memories (engine/migrations/036_holds.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryHolds.cs): families of instruction
  phrases and hidden-character signals, each counted once by its weight, a hold at a score of 1.0, text normalised
  before phrases are matched, text over 32 KiB held as too long with its head and tail scanned, and no automatic
  release, after Second Brain's quarantine (src/quarantine/score.ts, src/quarantine/hold.ts,
  src/quarantine/normalize.ts); the patterns are Rhythm's own.
- Write bursts (engine/migrations/045_quarantine.sql): a login's memory writes after its 40th in ten minutes held for a
  person whatever their words, only content writes counted, and instruction phrases left unscored on a person's own
  channel while hidden characters still count, after Second Brain's burst rule and channel factors
  (src/quarantine/burst.ts, src/quarantine/score.ts); one login as one actor, which of Rhythm's channels are a person's
  own, an import scored as an AI client's and a held project kept out of every list are Rhythm's own.
- Sharing a memory (engine/migrations/048_memory_share.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryShare.cs): a share that moves the
  memory rather than copying it, the move and its audit row committed together, and a move to where the memory already
  is answering unchanged, after Second Brain's share (src/capture/share.ts); taking a memory back only to its author's
  private scope, never one no person wrote, a held memory shared only once a person releases it, and the move kept as a
  version are Rhythm's own.
- The audit trails (engine/migrations/037_admin_audit.sql, engine/src/Core/AdminAudit.cs): an insert-only trail of
  setup changes beside the memory trail, each row naming who made it and through which channel, and an AI client's
  own name cleaned of control characters and angle brackets, white space folded and cut to a fixed length rather
  than refused, after Second Brain's admin and memory audit (src/lib/admin-audit.ts, src/lib/audit.ts,
  src/mcp/client-label.ts); the events and their triggers are Rhythm's own.
- The Second Brain export importer (engine/src/Cli/SecondBrainImport.cs, engine/migrations/059_second_brain_import.sql):
  reading a file Second Brain exports (version 3, and the older shapes its own importer still reads), each row checked
  as its importer checks one, and its hold reasons read as Rhythm's where Rhythm holds alike, after Second Brain's
  export, import and quarantine tags (src/routes/entries.ts, src/entries/import.ts, src/quarantine/tags.ts); a new id
  for every record with its own kept in an origin map, nothing matched by title, the scope map, and every field the
  file lacks named in the report are Rhythm's own.
- Prompt capsules (engine/src/Core/Capsules.cs, engine/migrations/039_capsules.sql): fixed core and project slots in a
  fixed order, canonical memories only, a slot two memories fill reported rather than chosen, a 12 000-character
  budget whose sections are kept whole as a prefix of the slot order, text with no ids or times, its SHA-256 and a
  strong ETag of the whole answer, and at most 200 candidates, after Second Brain's prompt capsules
  (src/prompt-capsule/types.ts, select.ts, serialize.ts, etag.ts, build.ts); the slots as typed columns, the missing
  slots and the report's shape are Rhythm's own.
- Undo for native memories (engine/migrations/041_memory_undo.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryUndo.cs): the newest change
  or a numbered version undone as a change of its own, so an undo is undone the same way, reaching only the history
  the caller sees, a person's own newest changes theirs to undo, and an AI client's burst of changes undone as a
  group a few memories a call, its members found again by the server from an opaque key and undone only while the
  group's changes are still a memory's newest, after Second Brain's undo and change groups (src/memory/undo.ts,
  src/memory/versions.ts, src/brief/changes.ts); the hold rules and the key are Rhythm's own.
- The memory trash (engine/migrations/038_memory_trash.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryTrash.cs): a forgotten memory kept
  with its text and versions, restore as the trash's own undo with no version written, every step that consumes a
  trash row naming its nonce so a stale view never acts on a row purged, restored or trashed again since, delete
  forever never through an AI client's channel and leaving an audit trail with none of the memory's words, and a purge
  bounded per run, after Second Brain's trash (src/memory/trash.ts, src/memory/trash-list.ts, src/memory/cleanup.ts);
  the memory kept in place, its links kept in the trash row and the retention given again on a restore are Rhythm's own.
- The opt-in recall log (engine/migrations/042_recall_log.sql, engine/src/Core/RecallLog.cs): the question kept as
  asked, the daily cap checked inside the write, a few expired rows purged on each write, a follow-up window of 30
  minutes and only the latest recall marked followed, after Second Brain's sampled recall log (src/recall/log.ts); the
  rows and counts kept per reader's scope, apart from the memory rows, and only while the log is on are Rhythm's own.
- The memory tools (engine/src/Core/Mcp/MemoryTools.cs, engine/src/Core/Mcp/ToolText.cs): the tool names remember,
  append, update, get, list_recent, set_status, forget, undo, history, list_projects and list_teams with their
  arguments, a held memory listed by id without its words, the trash listed with the days left and restored by undo,
  and the behaviour asked of an AI client in the descriptions (one memory per thing worth finding, append over a near
  copy, get before relying on a truncated preview, forget only when the person asks), after Second Brain's MCP server
  (src/mcp/server.ts); the answers as JSON with output schemas, the channel fixed for every tool and the refusals of a
  release, a delete forever and a capsule slot through them are Rhythm's own.
- Truth over time (engine/migrations/049_truth.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryTruth.cs): when a memory was true as a window
  from its start to its end, windows compared rather than arrival order before one memory replaces another, the states
  current, replaced, ended and retracted, and the memories a retracted one had replaced reopened and those built on it
  flagged, undone when it comes back, after Second Brain's validity windows and retraction hooks
  (src/memory/validity.ts, src/recall/validity-view.ts); the plan and its confirmation in place of a model's verdict,
  the flags table and the cascade as a trigger on the memory trail are Rhythm's own.
- The link vocabulary (engine/src/Core/LinkTypes.cs, engine/migrations/051_typed_links.sql): the eight edge types by
  their names, which of them has no direction, the rule that decided and follows join two episodic memories only with
  an unclassified end never qualifying, and the three provenances, after Second Brain's edge types (src/graph/types.ts,
  src/graph/edges.ts); the provenances read as Rhythm's verifications, the rule kept by the map for the links Rhythm
  states while a source's own links stay as stated, and a link's confidence, source revision and checked time are
  Rhythm's own.
- Links a person or an AI client makes (engine/migrations/052_made_links.sql, engine/src/Core/MemoryLinks.cs): the link,
